1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,686
Aarav's bicycle has a small
dynamo that powers its lamp.

2
00:00:04,036 --> 00:00:06,355
Pedal fast, and the lamp blazes.

3
00:00:06,355 --> 00:00:08,263
Pedal slow, and it fades.

4
00:00:08,613 --> 00:00:11,555
The dynamo voltage keeps
changing with his speed.

5
00:00:11,905 --> 00:00:13,405
A phone would hate that.

6
00:00:13,405 --> 00:00:15,350
How do we hold a voltage steady?

7
00:00:15,950 --> 00:00:19,727
Think of a water tank with an
overflow pipe near the top.

8
00:00:20,077 --> 00:00:24,013
Pour in more water, and the extra
just spills out of the overflow.

9
00:00:24,363 --> 00:00:26,404
Pour in less, and less spills.

10
00:00:26,404 --> 00:00:28,399
The water level stays the same.

11
00:00:28,999 --> 00:00:32,728
In electronics, that overflow
pipe is a Zener diode.

12
00:00:33,078 --> 00:00:36,957
Its symbol is a diode
with a bent bar, like a Z.

13
00:00:37,307 --> 00:00:41,240
We connect it backwards, in
reverse bias, on purpose.

14
00:00:41,590 --> 00:00:45,204
A normal diode can be damaged
if pushed too hard backwards.

15
00:00:45,554 --> 00:00:47,214
A Zener is built for it.

16
00:00:47,214 --> 00:00:48,866
It is very heavily doped.

17
00:00:49,516 --> 00:00:50,921
Look at its graph.

18
00:00:50,921 --> 00:00:53,570
Forward, it acts like a normal diode.

19
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:58,346
Backwards, almost no current
flows, until one special voltage.

20
00:00:58,696 --> 00:01:01,120
That is the Zener voltage, V z.

21
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:02,614
The breakdown point.

22
00:01:02,964 --> 00:01:05,660
Past V z, the current can shoot up a lot.

23
00:01:06,010 --> 00:01:08,044
But the voltage barely moves.

24
00:01:08,044 --> 00:01:09,783
It stays stuck at V z.

25
00:01:10,433 --> 00:01:13,173
Now build a regulator on the breadboard.

26
00:01:13,523 --> 00:01:18,029
A changing input, a series resistor
R, and the Zener across the load.

27
00:01:18,379 --> 00:01:21,509
The load gets whatever
voltage the Zener holds.

28
00:01:21,509 --> 00:01:22,617
That is V z.

29
00:01:22,967 --> 00:01:24,431
Let the input rise.

30
00:01:24,431 --> 00:01:26,464
More current flows through R.

31
00:01:26,814 --> 00:01:30,753
The Zener soaks up the extra
current, like the overflow pipe.

32
00:01:31,103 --> 00:01:32,697
Now let the input fall.

33
00:01:32,697 --> 00:01:34,573
The Zener takes less current.

34
00:01:34,923 --> 00:01:37,686
Through it all, the load
voltage stays at V z.

35
00:01:38,336 --> 00:01:40,797
A Zener in breakdown holds its voltage.

36
00:01:40,797 --> 00:01:42,817
Extra current spills through it.

37
00:01:43,617 --> 00:01:46,083
Let's find every current, step by step.

38
00:01:46,433 --> 00:01:51,201
The resistor R takes the
leftover voltage, V in minus V z.

39
00:01:51,901 --> 00:01:55,895
So the current through R
is V in minus V z, over R.

40
00:01:56,595 --> 00:02:00,219
The load current, IL, is V z over RL.

41
00:02:00,919 --> 00:02:02,790
The Zener takes the rest.

42
00:02:02,790 --> 00:02:04,683
I z equals I minus IL.

43
00:02:05,383 --> 00:02:07,690
Zener power is V z times I z.

44
00:02:07,690 --> 00:02:09,595
Keep it below the rating.

45
00:02:10,245 --> 00:02:12,651
Two rules keep the regulator working.

46
00:02:13,001 --> 00:02:13,901
One.

47
00:02:13,504 --> 00:02:17,371
The input must stay above V
z, or the Zener switches off.

48
00:02:17,721 --> 00:02:18,621
Two.

49
00:02:18,190 --> 00:02:22,165
Zener current must stay between a
small minimum and a safe maximum.

50
00:02:22,765 --> 00:02:24,473
Too little, and it stops holding.

51
00:02:24,473 --> 00:02:25,921
Too much, and it overheats.

52
00:02:26,571 --> 00:02:27,712
Worked example.

53
00:02:27,712 --> 00:02:29,649
The input is fifteen volts.

54
00:02:29,649 --> 00:02:31,433
The Zener is nine volts.

55
00:02:31,783 --> 00:02:33,537
R is two hundred ohms.

56
00:02:33,537 --> 00:02:35,396
The load is one kilo-ohm.

57
00:02:35,746 --> 00:02:38,198
I is fifteen minus nine, over two hundred.

58
00:02:38,198 --> 00:02:39,779
That is thirty milliamperes.

59
00:02:40,379 --> 00:02:42,391
IL is nine over one thousand.

60
00:02:42,391 --> 00:02:43,568
Nine milliamperes.

61
00:02:44,168 --> 00:02:46,029
So I z is thirty minus nine.

62
00:02:46,029 --> 00:02:47,495
Twenty one milliamperes.

63
00:02:48,095 --> 00:02:51,072
Zener power is nine volts
times twenty one milliamperes.

64
00:02:51,672 --> 00:02:54,164
That is about zero point one nine watts.

65
00:02:54,764 --> 00:02:56,974
At eighteen volts in, I z grows.

66
00:02:56,974 --> 00:02:58,653
The load still gets nine.

67
00:02:59,303 --> 00:03:00,765
Now, the exam trap.

68
00:03:01,115 --> 00:03:03,940
Many students assume the
Zener is always at V z.

69
00:03:04,290 --> 00:03:05,310
First check it.

70
00:03:05,310 --> 00:03:07,797
Remove the Zener and
find the load voltage.

71
00:03:08,147 --> 00:03:12,404
Only if that is above V z does
the Zener break down and regulate.

72
00:03:13,054 --> 00:03:14,466
Where is this asked?

73
00:03:14,466 --> 00:03:15,680
PUC board papers.

74
00:03:16,030 --> 00:03:20,441
JEE Main asks Zener regulator currents
often, like January twenty twenty

75
00:03:20,441 --> 00:03:21,341
four.

76
00:03:21,351 --> 00:03:25,661
Expect to find the Zener current,
the series resistor, or the power.

77
00:03:26,311 --> 00:03:27,211
Try this.

78
00:03:27,099 --> 00:03:30,366
A six volt Zener has a
hundred ohm series resistor.

79
00:03:30,716 --> 00:03:34,392
The input is ten volts, the
load three hundred ohms.

80
00:03:34,392 --> 00:03:36,090
Find the Zener current.

81
00:03:41,690 --> 00:03:42,590
B.

82
00:03:41,947 --> 00:03:42,847
Twenty milliamperes.

83
00:03:43,155 --> 00:03:44,055
First check.

84
00:03:44,040 --> 00:03:47,462
Without the Zener, the load
gets seven point five volts.

85
00:03:47,812 --> 00:03:50,948
That is above six, so the
Zener regulates at six volts.

86
00:03:51,298 --> 00:03:53,674
I is ten minus six, over one hundred.

87
00:03:53,674 --> 00:03:54,844
Forty milliamperes.

88
00:03:55,194 --> 00:03:57,104
IL is six over three hundred.

89
00:03:57,104 --> 00:03:58,310
Twenty milliamperes.

90
00:03:58,660 --> 00:04:00,686
So I z is forty minus twenty.

91
00:04:00,686 --> 00:04:01,994
Twenty milliamperes.

92
00:04:02,644 --> 00:04:04,191
Screenshot this card.

93
00:04:07,391 --> 00:04:11,428
Next, diodes that work with light:
the light emitting diode, photodiode

94
00:04:11,428 --> 00:04:12,473
and solar cell.
